FILE /usr/share/man/cat1/voice.1
    voice: runs a terminal program in all but the terminal's last rows, where it
    shows what it hears, gives the page buttons that a finger taps (touch@0),
    and types what each says, or what is said, into the program:
    transcribe.cpp with NVIDIA's Parakeet model.
    
      voice MENU [MODEL.gguf] -- PROGRAM [ARG...]
    
    In MENU, "[NAME] caption" begins a page of buttons and "LABEL = TEXT" is a
    button that types TEXT (\r Enter, \e Escape, \t Tab, \xHH a byte, \w a wait
    of 0.4 s). After the text, :speak listens and types what was said and
    :page NAME shows another page. pi-phone's /etc/dolly/pi.menu is one.
    PROGRAM runs on a pseudo-terminal; the keyboard and a paste still reach it,
    and PROGRAM may ask voice for the same, a line each, on the pipe
    VOICE_CONTROL_FD names: page NAME, type TEXT. It needs touch@0,
    pty@0 and microphone@0; the
    microphone is open only while it listens, and nothing that is said leaves
    the page.
